Yep — dorking as deliberate query construction: using operators, syntax, odd keyword combinations, exclusions, filetypes, cached/indexed traces, etc., to make a search engine expose structure that ordinary search hides.

The part that fits our broader work is the abstraction: don’t search harder; alter the query-space so the hidden thing becomes easy to retrieve. That generalizes well beyond Google—repositories, databases, memories, stories, logs, even conversations. A “dork” is basically a purpose-built lens.